<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Umbra Reader talks to a server the reader runs themselves — on a LAN
  address, a Tailscale hostname, or localhost — and those are almost always
  plain HTTP. Android has refused cleartext by default since API 28, which
  makes that the normal case rather than the exceptional one.

  This surfaced as a puzzle worth recording: browsing the library worked and
  downloading a volume did not. Dart's HttpClient opens its own sockets and
  never consults this policy, so every request the Dart side makes went
  through. background_downloader hands the transfer to Android's own HTTP
  stack, which does consult it — so the catalogue loaded and the books would
  not come down.

  Permitted broadly rather than by host: the address belongs to the reader,
  is typed in at runtime, and can be an IP literal, a MagicDNS name, or a
  hostname on their own network. Android's config matches exact domains and
  cannot express "any private range", so an allow-list here would be a list
  of guesses that breaks the first time someone moves their server.

  If you would rather not send credentials in the clear at all, the fix is at
  the server end rather than here: Tailscale Serve can put HTTPS in front of
  Novel Grabber, after which this policy stops mattering.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
